      "type": "text",
      "text": "I recommended pausing the run.\n\n- **What I saw:** pressure held at about 3.05 Torr through the ramp. During growth it rose steadily from 3.08 to 3.38 Torr between t=2520 s and t=3120 s. Argon flow stayed at 100 sccm, temperature held at 750 °C and heater power stayed at about 44%.\n- **Why the cause is open:** both a blocked exhaust and an air leak would push pressure up in a tube running below atmospheric pressure. A leak is the bigger risk at 750 °C, because air getting in could oxidise the sample.\n- **Missing sensor:** the growth stage requires an exhaust O2 sensor to confirm the atmosphere, and this furnace doesn't have one.\n- **What would settle it:** a leak test (close the valves and watch how fast pressure rises) or a check of the exhaust line and pump.\n\nThere's no characterisation of the sample yet, so there's nothing to say about whether the material grew. If it's measured later, the result shouldn't count as evidence about the recipe unless the pressure rise turns out to be harmless."
     }
